 ## Issues in Your Approach

Your logic is mostly right, but there are **two bugs**:

---

### Bug 1: `usedLvl` is never cleared between levels

You remove words from `st` that were used *in the current level*, but `usedLvl` keeps accumulating across all levels. This means words from **previous levels** get removed again on the next iteration — which is harmless but also means words added *during* the current level processing aren't batched correctly.

**The real problem:** you add to `usedLvl` immediately as you find neighbors, but you only remove them from `st` when the *next* level starts. This is actually the correct BFS ladder approach — but `usedLvl` needs to be **reset after each flush**, otherwise it grows unboundedly and you lose track of what belongs to the current level.

```java
// After flushing usedLvl into st removals, clear it:
if (v.size() > lvl) {
    lvl++;
    for (String it : usedLvl) {
        st.remove(it);
    }
    usedLvl.clear(); // ← ADD THIS
}
```

---

### Bug 2: You add `replacedWord` to `usedLvl` even if the same word appears multiple times in the same level

This causes duplicate removals and potentially skips valid paths. Use a **local set per level flush** or deduplicate `usedLvl`:

Since `usedLvl` feeds into `st.remove()` (which is idempotent), duplicates in `usedLvl` don't corrupt results — but they waste memory. The `clear()` fix above handles this implicitly.

---

### Bug 3 (subtle): `v.add(replacedWord)` mutates the current path vector

You do:
```java
v.add(replacedWord);
ArrayList<String> temp = new ArrayList<>(v); // copy after mutation
v.remove(v.size() - 1);                      // undo mutation
```

This works **only if `v` isn't shared** — and since you dequeue a fresh `ArrayList` each time, it's safe here. But it's fragile. The cleaner pattern:

```java
// Build temp directly without mutating v
ArrayList<String> temp = new ArrayList<>(v);
temp.add(replacedWord);
q.add(temp);
```

---

### Corrected Code

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> findLadders(String beginWord, String endWord, List<String> wordList) {
        Set<String> st = new HashSet<>(wordList);
        Queue<ArrayList<String>> q = new LinkedList<>();

        ArrayList<String> ls = new ArrayList<>();
        ls.add(beginWord);
        q.add(ls);

        List<String> usedLvl = new ArrayList<>();
        usedLvl.add(beginWord);

        int lvl = 0;
        List<List<String>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            ArrayList<String> v = q.poll();

            // Moving to next level: flush used words from set
            if (v.size() > lvl) {
                lvl++;
                for (String it : usedLvl) st.remove(it);
                usedLvl.clear(); // ← fix #1
            }

            String word = v.get(v.size() - 1);

            if (word.equals(endWord)) {
                if (ans.isEmpty() || ans.get(0).size() == v.size())
                    ans.add(v);
                continue;
            }

            for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
                char[] arr = word.toCharArray();
                for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {
                    arr[i] = ch;
                    String replacedWord = new String(arr);
                    if (st.contains(replacedWord)) {
                        ArrayList<String> temp = new ArrayList<>(v); // ← fix #3
                        temp.add(replacedWord);
                        q.add(temp);
                        usedLvl.add(replacedWord);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

---

