 ## Root Cause of TLE

The BFS is correct now, but you're storing **entire paths** in the queue. For a word of length `L` with `N` words, you can have exponentially many paths queued. This blows up both time and memory.

---

## Better Approach: BFS for levels + DFS for path reconstruction

Instead of storing paths in the queue, store a **parent map** — for each word, track which words led to it at the previous BFS level. Then reconstruct all paths via DFS at the end.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> findLadders(String beginWord, String endWord, List<String> wordList) {
        Set<String> st = new HashSet<>(wordList);
        // word -> list of parent words that reached it
        Map<String, List<String>> parents = new HashMap<>();

        Set<String> currentLevel = new HashSet<>();
        currentLevel.add(beginWord);

        boolean found = false;
        st.remove(beginWord);

        // BFS level by level
        while (!currentLevel.isEmpty() && !found) {
            Set<String> nextLevel = new HashSet<>();

            // Remove current level words from set BEFORE processing
            // so siblings can still reach the same word in this level
            for (String word : currentLevel) st.remove(word);

            for (String word : currentLevel) {
                char[] arr = word.toCharArray();
                for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
                    char original = arr[i];
                    for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {
                        arr[i] = ch;
                        String next = new String(arr);
                        if (st.contains(next)) {
                            nextLevel.add(next);
                            // record 'word' as a parent of 'next'
                            parents.computeIfAbsent(next, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(word);
                            if (next.equals(endWord)) found = true;
                        }
                    }
                    arr[i] = original; // restore
                }
            }
            currentLevel = nextLevel;
        }

        List<List<String>> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        if (!found) return ans;

        // DFS to reconstruct all paths from endWord back to beginWord
        LinkedList<String> path = new LinkedList<>();
        path.addFirst(endWord);
        dfs(beginWord, endWord, parents, path, ans);
        return ans;
    }

    private void dfs(String beginWord, String word, Map<String, List<String>> parents,
                     LinkedList<String> path, List<List<String>> ans) {
        if (word.equals(beginWord)) {
            ans.add(new ArrayList<>(path));
            return;
        }
        if (!parents.containsKey(word)) return;
        for (String parent : parents.get(word)) {
            path.addFirst(parent);
            dfs(beginWord, parent, parents, path, ans);
            path.removeFirst();
        }
    }
}
```

---

## Why this is faster

| | Your approach | This approach |
|---|---|---|
| Queue stores | Full paths | Just words (one per node) |
| Memory | O(paths × path_length) — exponential | O(N × neighbors) — linear |
| Duplicate work | Same word re-expanded per path | Each word expanded exactly once |
| Key insight | — | Siblings in same BFS level **can share** the same `next` word via `parents` map |

---

## Key trick: when to remove from `st`

Remove the **entire current level** from `st` **before** iterating — not word by word. This allows two words in the same BFS level to both point to the same `next` word (valid shortest paths), while still preventing going backwards to previous levels.

```java
