 Sure, breaking it down line by line:

**`bloodBankSchema.path("reviews")`**
```javascript
// .path() accesses a specific field on the schema
// Here it's targeting the entire "reviews" array
bloodBankSchema.path("reviews")
```

---

**`.validate(function(reviews), errorMessage)`**
```javascript
// Mongoose's custom validator
// "reviews" here is the entire array being validated
// Runs before every .save()
.validate(function (reviews) { ... }, "A user can only submit one review")
```

---

**Inside the validator:**
```javascript
// Step 1 — extract all userIds as plain strings
const ids = reviews.map(r => r.userId.toString());
// e.g. ["abc123", "xyz789", "abc123"]  ← abc123 appears twice = duplicate

// Step 2 — Set automatically removes duplicates
new Set(ids)
// e.g. Set { "abc123", "xyz789" }  ← now only 2 items

// Step 3 — compare lengths
return ids.length === new Set(ids).size;
// 3 === 2 → false → validation FAILS → error thrown
// 2 === 2 → true  → validation PASSES → save proceeds
```

---

**Why `.toString()`?**
```javascript
// MongoDB ObjectIds are objects, not strings
// Two ObjectId objects with the same value are NOT equal with ===
r.userId === r.userId   // false ← compares object references
r.userId.toString() === r.userId.toString()  // true ← compares actual ID values

// Set uses === internally, so without .toString() duplicates won't be detected
```

---

**Full flow visualised:**
```
User "abc123" submits review → reviews array:
[
  { userId: "abc123", rating: 5 },
  { userId: "xyz789", rating: 3 },
  { userId: "abc123", rating: 1 }  ← duplicate!
]

ids     = ["abc123", "xyz789", "abc123"]  length = 3
new Set = { "abc123", "xyz789" }          size   = 2

3 === 2 → false → ❌ "A user can only submit one review"
```

